{:edb/db-before-t 1683 :edb/db-after-t 1684 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534996 50 #inst "2026-10-08T13:45:37.670Z" 13194139534996 true] [17592186124296 1100 "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf{F}$, where $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2xy\\ \\mathbf i + (x^2 - 3)\\ \\mathbf j$." 13194139534996 false] [17592186124296 1100 "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $\\nabla f = \\mathbf{F},$ where $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 2xy\\ \\mathbf i + (x^2 - 3)\\ \\mathbf j .$" 13194139534996 true] [17592186124296 1145 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}\\,\\textbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}}\\,\\textbf{j}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf F(x,y)\n$$\n\nthe function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2-3\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y) &= ∫\\frac{∂f}{∂x}dx \\\\\n&= ∫2xydx \\\\\n&= x^{2}y + g(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 + g'(y)\n$$\n\nTaking\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 -3\n$$\n\ninto account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + g^{′}(y) &= x^{2} - 3 \\\\\ng^{′}(y) &= -3 \\\\\ng(y) &= -3y + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\ng(y)=-3y +C\n$$\n\ninto our expression for $f(x,y)$, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2y -3y + C\n$$" 13194139534996 false] [17592186124296 1145 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}\\,\\textbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}}\\,\\textbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = 2xy, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2-3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2xy\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}y+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 + g'(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = x^2 -3$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & ={x}^{2}-3 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =-3 \\\\ g(y) & =-3y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=-3y +C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^2y -3y + C.\n$$" 13194139534996 true] [17592186124300 1100 "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)  = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = yz\\,\\mathbf{i} + (xz + 4z^2)\\,\\mathbf{j} + (xy+8yz)\\,\\mathbf{k}.$" 13194139534996 true] [17592186124300 1100 "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)$, where $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = yz\\,\\mathbf{i} + (xz + 4z^2)\\,\\mathbf{j} + (xy+8yz)\\,\\mathbf{k}$." 13194139534996 false] [17592186124300 1145 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} \\, \\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)\n$$\n\nthe function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = yz, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x, y, z) &= ∫\\frac{∂f}{∂x}dx \\\\\n&= ∫yzdx \\\\\n&= xyz + g(y, z)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + \\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}\n$$\n\nTaking\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2\n$$\n\ninto account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nxz + \\frac{∂g}{∂y} &= xz + 4z^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{∂g}{∂y} &= 4z^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}\n$$\n\nwith respect to $y$. Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y, z) &= ∫\\frac{∂g}{∂y}dy \\\\\n&= ∫4z^{2}dy \\\\\n&= 4yz^{2} + h(z)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ng(y,z) = 4yz^2 + h(z)\n$$\n\nin the expression for $f(x, y,z)$, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + h(z)\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z)$, we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy + 8yz + h'(z)\n$$\n\nTaking\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz\n$$\n\ninto account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nxy + 8yz &= xy + 8yz + h^{′}(z) \\\\\nh^{′}(z) &= 0 \\\\\nh(z) &= C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + C\n$$" 13194139534996 false] [17592186124300 1145 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} \\, \\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)  = \\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}} = yz, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz.\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{x}}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int yz\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =xyz+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + \\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{y}} = xz + 4z^2$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xz+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =xz+4{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =4{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\dfrac{\\partial{g}}{\\partial{y}}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 4{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z) = 4yz^2 + h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x, y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy + 8yz + h'(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\dfrac{\\partial{f}}{\\partial{z}} = xy+8yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy+8yz & =xy+8yz+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z) = xyz + 4yz^2 + C.\n$$" 13194139534996 true] [17592186124490 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325576 13194139534996 true] [17592186124490 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325583 13194139534996 true] [17592186124490 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325604 13194139534996 true] [17592186124490 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325611 13194139534996 true] [17592186124495 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325569 13194139534996 true] [17592186124495 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325590 13194139534996 true] [17592186124495 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325597 13194139534996 true] [17592186124495 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325618 13194139534996 true] [17592186124495 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325625 13194139534996 true] [17592186124859 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534996 true] [17592186124859 1100 "Find $f(x, y)$ such that $∇f = F$, where $F(x, y) =-yi + (3 - x)j$." 13194139534996 false] [17592186124859 1100 "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=-y\\,i+(3-x)\\,j.$" 13194139534996 true] [17592186124859 1145 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3-x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (-y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-xy+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3-x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+{g}^{′}(y) & =3-x \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =3 \\\\ g(y) & =3y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=3y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=-xy+3y+C.\n$$" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325569 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325570 13194139534996 true] [17592186325569 1089 "q-124729" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325569 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534996 true] [17592186325569 1100 "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(2xz+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j+{x}^{2}\\,k.$" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325569 1113 #uuid "37a4f167-d075-5cc5-b42f-1d28c266ec7c" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325569 1145 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xz+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2xz+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={x}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C.\n$$" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325571 13194139534996 true] [17592186325570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325572 13194139534996 true] [17592186325570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325573 13194139534996 true] [17592186325570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325574 13194139534996 true] [17592186325570 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325575 13194139534996 true] [17592186325570 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534996 true] [17592186325570 1041 "selection" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325570 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325571 13194139534996 true] [17592186325570 1110 #uuid "59f9a68f-8e05-5cc7-80f5-482098347c63" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325571 1034 "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325571 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534996 true] [17592186325571 1111 #uuid "850ffa8c-c7b2-550b-98db-3c89c5f951dc" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325572 1034 "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325572 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534996 true] [17592186325572 1111 #uuid "149ea04e-66f8-5e19-b4d9-259d197a3f4a" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325573 1034 "f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+yz+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325573 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534996 true] [17592186325573 1111 #uuid "9cbe9721-a32a-505a-b844-9c3331e21d26" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325574 1034 "f(x,y,z)=xy+{x}^{2}z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+C" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325574 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534996 true] [17592186325574 1111 #uuid "70a63e5d-bbb0-511f-8c29-b51f3ec807a4" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325575 1034 "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+C" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325575 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534996 true] [17592186325575 1111 #uuid "051d5421-f4e3-52b3-a545-d88a40476684" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325576 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325577 13194139534996 true] [17592186325576 1089 "q-132077" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325576 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534996 true] [17592186325576 1100 "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x)\\,j.$" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325576 1113 #uuid "68e41e8c-a69a-5d43-a9b3-91bf34e5d4cc" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325576 1145 "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+xy+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking  $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x+{g}^{′}(y) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+x \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ g(y) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+xy+C.\n$$" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325577 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325578 13194139534996 true] [17592186325577 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325579 13194139534996 true] [17592186325577 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325580 13194139534996 true] [17592186325577 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325581 13194139534996 true] [17592186325577 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325582 13194139534996 true] [17592186325577 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534996 true] [17592186325577 1041 "selection" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325577 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325581 13194139534996 true] [17592186325577 1110 #uuid "ed5ee9ad-6605-5b15-97b7-79e7cb030682" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325578 1034 "f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}+C" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325578 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534996 true] [17592186325578 1111 #uuid "32204b71-af4f-57d4-8085-6de44422b55c" 13194139534996 true] [17592186325579 1034 "f(x,y)=\\operator